---
title: "6 · Saved signatures and signing mandates"
description: How the firm's own signature is applied to contracts from a saved signature, and where it is set up.
sidebar:
  order: 7
  label: "6 · Saved signatures and signing mandates"
---

On contracts sent from CRM, the firm does not sign through an email link. Its signatory signs once in advance: a tenant admin registers the signatory's **saved signature** (and, optionally, the issuing entity's company seal) and ticks a statement that authorises Operon to apply it. That authorisation is the signing mandate.

## Where it is used

- Engagement letters and quotations: the firm's saved signature is applied before the contract is sent, so the client receives a copy that already carries it.
- VIP contracts: once the client has signed, Operon countersigns automatically with the saved signature, and with the company seal when one is registered.

Saved signatures are used only for the firm's side of a CRM contract. A request sent from **+ New Request** or from a case is always signed by each signer through their own link.

## Set it up

A tenant admin adds the saved signature in CRM settings, on the contract signatory's record: see [CRM · Contract templates](/en/crm/templates/).

Changing the signatory's printed name or deactivating the signatory revokes the saved signature. Contracts cannot be sent until a new signature is added; contracts already signed keep their signature.

## How it shows on a request

On the request's page, the firm's signatory appears as a signer. Point at them on **By document** to see "Signature: Saved signature".
